{
  "rules": [
    "Systematic Underestimation of Bullish Spikes: The model consistently fails to capture rapid price surges (e.g., the rise from ~68 to ~80 in Dec 2023 and ~70 to ~76 in May 2024), often predicting a flat or slightly upward trend while the market rallies sharply. Correction: Apply a positive momentum multiplier when the 3-day price change exceeds 2% or when sentiment history shows consecutive positive values.",
    "Delayed Mean Reversion (Lag Effect): Forecasts exhibit a 'flatline' bias, failing to anticipate the subsequent sharp decline after a peak. The model predicts a slow, linear drift rather than the observed volatility and rapid reversion to the mean. Correction: Introduce a negative correction factor in the 10-15 day horizon following any price increase of >5% over the previous 5 days.",
    "Sentiment Ignorance: The model ignores sentiment history (e.g., positive sentiment spikes in Dec 2023 and May 2024) and continues to output near-constant forecasts. Correction: Weight the forecast trajectory by the average sentiment score of the last 5 days; if average sentiment > 0.2, increase the forecast slope by 0.5-1.0 EUR/day.",
    "Over-smoothing of Volatility: The model's 30-day forecast is too smooth, missing the amplitude of the true outcome's oscillations. Correction: Increase the forecast variance by scaling the standard deviation of the last 10 days of history by 1.5x to capture the true market volatility.",
    "Horizon-Specific Bias: The model is most accurate at the immediate next-day horizon but diverges significantly by day 5-10. Correction: Apply a 'decay' function to the forecast slope, reducing the predicted daily increment by 50% after day 3 to prevent over-prediction during stable periods."
  ]
}
